1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:05,340
Captions make a video easier to follow, even with the sound off.

2
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:12,952
In this tutorial, I'll add captions to a short clip using Shotcut, a free and open-source video editor.

3
00:00:13,352 --> 00:00:18,921
We'll import the clip, add captions, check the timing, move them into place, and export.

4
00:00:19,871 --> 00:00:23,158
Click Open File, choose your video, and press Enter.

5
00:00:23,208 --> 00:00:25,236
The clip opens in the player.

6
00:00:28,954 --> 00:00:33,137
Click the plus button in the timeline to add the clip to your project.

7
00:00:33,637 --> 00:00:38,204
Next, click Subtitles in the toolbar to open the Subtitles panel.

8
00:00:38,704 --> 00:00:42,972
Click the plus button at the top to create a new subtitle track.

9
00:00:43,022 --> 00:00:46,415
Name it, set the language to English, and click OK.

10
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:50,591
Now move the playhead to where the first line starts.

11
00:00:50,641 --> 00:00:53,864
Click Add, and type the caption into the Current box.

12
00:00:55,327 --> 00:01:00,000
To end the caption, move the playhead to the end of the line, and click Set End.

13
00:01:00,614 --> 00:01:03,410
Repeat the same steps for the next two lines.

14
00:01:11,457 --> 00:01:15,149
To show the captions on the video, click the Burn In button.

15
00:01:15,199 --> 00:01:18,635
It adds a Subtitle Burn In filter to the output.

16
00:01:19,135 --> 00:01:23,659
Play it back from the start, and check that each caption matches what you hear.

17
00:01:28,965 --> 00:01:34,918
If a caption needs to start earlier, move the playhead, select the caption, and click Set Start.

18
00:01:35,368 --> 00:01:37,609
Now let's reposition the captions.

19
00:01:37,659 --> 00:01:42,908
In the Filters panel, the Subtitle Burn In filter controls position and style.

20
00:01:43,408 --> 00:01:47,036
I move them up a little by changing the vertical position.

21
00:01:47,086 --> 00:01:50,757
Then I make the outline thicker, so the text stays readable.

22
00:01:51,257 --> 00:01:53,434
When it looks right, click Export.

23
00:01:53,484 --> 00:02:01,528
Keep the Default preset, which makes an H.264 MP4 file, and click Export Video and Audio.

24
00:02:02,029 --> 00:02:04,270
Type a file name and press Enter.

25
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:07,244
Shotcut renders the video in the Jobs panel.

26
00:02:10,624 --> 00:02:14,465
When the job is done, open the new file to check the result.

27
00:02:14,515 --> 00:02:16,991
The captions are now part of the video.

28
00:02:25,256 --> 00:02:31,337
That's the whole process: import, add captions, check the timing, reposition, and export.

29
00:02:31,737 --> 00:02:33,338
Thanks for watching.
